<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  es-theme-xmb-psp - "Button Icons: PSP".
  Selected via UI Settings > Theme Configuration > Button Icons > PSP.

  The authentic set, and the reason audit S4 exists: the PSP labels its face
  buttons with shapes, not letters. Not the default only because it is
  actively misleading on a TrimUI Brick, whose buttons say A/B/X/Y — a strip
  reading "(x) SELECT" gives that user nothing to press. See the decision on
  #8.

  Slot -> physical position is fixed by ES (iconA=EAST, iconB=SOUTH,
  iconX=NORTH, iconY=WEST); the full derivation and the InvertButtons
  non-caveat are documented once in _inc/buttons-nintendo.xml. Sony's shapes
  sit on those positions as:

      EAST  = Circle    SOUTH = Cross    NORTH = Triangle    WEST = Square

  Note that Cross lands on iconB and Circle on iconA. Writing the obvious
  <iconA>cross.png</iconA> — as docs/psp-xmb-style-guidelines.md 5.4 did until
  this issue, and as the original text of #8 proposed — puts Cross on the east
  button and Circle on the south one, i.e. exactly backwards, on a theme whose
  whole point is PSP authenticity. Nothing errors; the glyphs simply appear on
  the wrong buttons.

  Which shape CONFIRMS is not this file's decision. The Brick ships
  InvertButtons=true, making east the confirm button, so out of the box this
  set gives Circle (iconA, east) = CONFIRM and Cross (iconB, south) = BACK —
  the JAPANESE PSP convention. Western firmware is the other way round, and a
  user who turns Invert Buttons off gets that instead, because the setting
  moves the CONFIRM and BACK labels between the two glyphs. Either way each
  shape stays on its own physical button, which is the part this file fixes.

  CC-BY-NC-SA 2.0. See CREDITS.md.
-->
<theme>
  <formatVersion>7</formatVersion>
  <variables>
    <helpIconA>./art/help/psp-circle.png</helpIconA>
    <helpIconB>./art/help/psp-cross.png</helpIconB>
    <helpIconX>./art/help/psp-triangle.png</helpIconX>
    <helpIconY>./art/help/psp-square.png</helpIconY>
  </variables>
</theme>
